<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/living-room.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/living-room-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/living-room.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Questions answered</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Answers</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Questions, Answered</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers grouped by topic: how appraisal works, what it costs, how long it takes and how South Dakota rules fit in.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-light" href="/get-a-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><nav class="toc" aria-label="FAQ topics"><a href="#faq-0">How appraisal works</a><a href="#faq-1">Costs and fees</a><a href="#faq-2">Timing and deadlines</a><a href="#faq-3">South Dakota specifics</a><a href="#faq-4">Working with us</a></nav><h2 id="faq-0">How appraisal works</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is insurance appraisal?</summary><div class="ans"><p>A process written into most property policies for settling disagreements about the amount of a covered loss. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and an agreement signed by any two of the three sets the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who can demand appraisal?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Under most policies, either you or the insurer can demand appraisal in writing when you disagree on the amount of loss.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the appraisal award binding?</summary><div class="ans"><p>On the amount of loss, generally yes, once two of the three panel members sign. Coverage questions are separate.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal decide coverage?</summary><div class="ans"><p>No. Appraisal values the loss. Whether the policy covers it is decided by the policy, the parties and, if necessary, the courts.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I need a lawyer for appraisal?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Not necessarily. Many appraisals happen without lawyers. If there is a coverage denial, a bad-faith concern or an approaching deadline, talk to a South Dakota attorney.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-1">Costs and fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays for appraisal?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Usually each side pays its own appraiser and the two sides split the umpire’s fee and other shared costs. Your policy controls.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do appraisers charge?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Arrangements vary: flat fees, hourly rates or a percentage of the increase. We put our fee in writing before any work begins.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it for a small claim?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Often not. When the gap is small, a documented supplement request is usually faster and cheaper. Our appraisal payoff estimator helps you check.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-2">Timing and deadlines</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does appraisal take?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Simple residential claims often resolve within a few months of both appraisers being named. Complex claims take longer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is there a deadline to demand appraisal?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Some policies set time limits around appraisal and all policies have a suit limitation clause. Read your policy and do not assume appraisal pauses any deadline.</p></div></details><details><summary>When do I get paid after an award?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Policies usually set a period for payment after an award or agreement. The carrier applies the deductible, limits and prior payments.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-3">South Dakota specifics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who regulates insurers in South Dakota?</summary><div class="ans"><p>The South Dakota Division of Insurance, part of the Department of Labor and Regulation, based in Pierre. It accepts consumer complaints about claim handling.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does South Dakota have a FAIR plan or wind pool?</summary><div class="ans"><p>No. South Dakota does not have a state FAIR plan or wind pool. Properties that standard carriers decline are typically placed in the surplus lines market.</p></div></details><details><summary>What are the most common claims here?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Hail and wind lead by a wide margin, followed by winter damage, water losses, fire and, in the Black Hills, wildfire.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if my insurer acted in bad faith?</summary><div class="ans"><p>South Dakota recognizes legal remedies when an insurer handles a claim unreasonably. Those are legal claims for an attorney. Appraisal deals only with the amount of loss.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-4">Working with us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do you work statewide?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Yes. We serve all 66 South Dakota counties, from the Sioux Empire to the Black Hills.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are you a public adjuster or a law firm?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Neither. We provide appraisal, estimating and umpire services. We do not negotiate claims outside appraisal and we do not give legal advice.</p></div></details><details><summary>What should I send for a first review?</summary><div class="ans"><p>The carrier’s full estimate, your declarations page, carrier letters, photos and your contractor’s estimate if you have one.</p></div></details></div></article>
<aside class="aside"><div class="box box-call"><h2 class="box-t">Talk it through first</h2><p>The first conversation is free. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="big-tel" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ink" href="/get-a-claim-review/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="box"><h2 class="box-t">Resources</h2><ul class="box-list"><li><a href="/inside-the-appraisal-process/">Inside the appraisal process</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-questions-answered/" aria-current="page">Appraisal questions answered</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal-glossary/">Appraisal glossary</a></li><li><a href="/storm-claim-articles/">Storm claim articles</a></li></ul></div></aside></div><section class="strip"><div class="wrap strip-in rv"><div><h2>Didn’t see your question?</h2><p>Call or email. We’re happy to talk it through.</p></div><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn ink" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line" href="/get-a-claim-review/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
